Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 00:38:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] test(r58): phase B proof cells - the reorder runs, the output
 does not move

Run after CHAIN138 END under HQ's LOAD-CLEAR, one process at a time, every
job detached with an in-turn wait. No perf or bench cell of any kind.

- llama-server built (chain139 serves from build-vulkan/bin), EXIT=0.
- GET_ROWS 151/151 OK twice.
- S-X8 27B legacy vs GGML_VK_HOST_SPLIT_MEMTYPE=3: all five host-split
  buffers move memtype 1 -> memtype 3 in the receipt lines while the
  generated text stays bit-identical (sha256 5fc09c5fded36c61..., 165 B both
  arms). The receipt assertion is what proves ggml_vk_host_split_prefer()
  actually ran - its early returns are silent, so identical text alone would
  also be consistent with the reorder never happening.
- GGML_VK_HOST_SPLIT_MAX=0 refuses before any buffer is allocated.
- Q4_K_XL loads with zero host-split lines.

NOT CLEAN, and stated rather than buried: the full MUL_MAT sweep is 2126/2128
then 2127/2128. q6_K at the 27B lm_head shape (248320x5120, n=1) returns a NaN
in BOTH sweeps at a DIFFERENT element index each time, and passes 12/12 in six
consecutive isolated runs; q5_0 at the same m failed once and passed on repeat.
A NaN that moves its index across runs and never reproduces alone is
state-dependent, not arithmetic. It cannot be this diff - with no switch set
the reorder is not entered and the candidate walk is byte-for-byte the shipped
one - but it is not provably pre-existing either, because no same-source
baseline test-backend-ops exists. Owed: build test-backend-ops at 50b0e9f7f and
run the same sweep twice. Until then this is an open F-class candidate against
the fork's q6_K mat-vec path, not a clean bill of health.

Origin: fork-authored (ArifiLabs lane-244 R58 UMA memory-type placement, Opus 5 MEDIUM maker)
Measured-effect: NONE measured by the maker - correctness and placement receipts only; bit-identical output across memory types 1 and 3; HQ chain139 owns every throughput number
Lane: lane-244
